Q: Why this code doesn&#39;t generate circles but instead does this? A: <blockquote>
<p>Why this code doesn't generate circles</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Primary issue:</p>
<blockquote>
<p>It looks as though you've ended up with a <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Moir%C3%A9_pattern" rel="nofollow noreferrer">moiré effect</a>. You've calculated the colour as if it were a single channel with 2²⁴ levels, not three channels each with 2⁸ levels. ... <a href="https://stackoverflow.com/questions/79865195/why-this-code-doesnt-generate-circles-but-instead-does-this#comment140936926_79865195">@
Weather Vane</a></p>
</blockquote>
<p>To achieve a grey scale, assigned the values [0...0xFF] to each primary.</p>
<pre><code>// Sawtooth grey scale.
data[i*PIXEL_COLS + j]  = (uint32_t) trunc(0xFF * cos_dis) * 0x010101;
</code></pre>
<p>To achieve a rainbow scale, assigned the values [0...0xFF] split over 2 pairs of  primaries.</p>
<pre><code>// Simplifications exists.  Verbose for clarity.
// Other color wheel models exist - the below is just one.
unsigned index  = trunc(0xFF*3 * cos_dis);
unsigned hue = index%0x100;
unsigned pair = index/0x100;
#define PRI3 0  /* or 0xFF */
uint32_t r,g,b;
switch(pair) {
  case 0: r = hue; g = PRI3; b = 0xFF-hue; break;
  case 1: r = 0xFF-hue; g = hue; b = PRI3; break;
  case 2: r = PRI3; g = 0xFF-hue; b = hue; break;
}
r &lt;&lt;= 0;
g &lt;&lt;= 8;
b &lt;&lt;= 16;
data[i*PIXEL_COLS + j]  = b | g | r;
</code></pre>

Q: Are flex items block level element? A: <p>There's this great single sentence explanation that I wish could be spread more</p>
<blockquote>
<p><a href="https://stackoverflow.com/questions/63873539/clarifying-definition-of-block-container-and-block-box#comment112953251_63874242">The </a><strong><a href="https://stackoverflow.com/questions/63873539/clarifying-definition-of-block-container-and-block-box#comment112953251_63874242">&quot;level&quot;-ness</a></strong><a href="https://stackoverflow.com/questions/63873539/clarifying-definition-of-block-container-and-block-box#comment112953251_63874242"> of a box describes how a box interacts with its ancestor and sibling boxes, whereas </a><strong><a href="https://stackoverflow.com/questions/63873539/clarifying-definition-of-block-container-and-block-box#comment112953251_63874242">&quot;container&quot;-ness</a></strong><a href="https://stackoverflow.com/questions/63873539/clarifying-definition-of-block-container-and-block-box#comment112953251_63874242"> describes how a box interacts with its descendant boxes</a></p>
</blockquote>
<p>So, according to what you have, &quot;a flex item is <strong>blockified</strong>&quot;, that means its <em>&quot;container&quot;-ness</em> becomes &quot;block&quot;, not its <em>&quot;level&quot;-ness</em>. Its &quot;level&quot;-ness is still &quot;flex&quot;. So flex items are <strong>flex-level</strong> <strong>block-container</strong> boxes.</p>
<pre class="lang-html prettyprint-override"><code>&lt;div style=&quot;display: flex&quot;&gt; &lt;!-- flex container --&gt;
    &lt;span&gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;!-- flex level, block container box (got blockified) --&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;!-- without flex formatting context --&gt;
&lt;div&gt; &lt;!-- block container --&gt;
    &lt;span&gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;!-- inline level box, NOT a block container --&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
</code></pre>
<p>Tho what's the practical implication of this? You can read more in this answer <a href="https://stackoverflow.com/a/11841648/9787887">https://stackoverflow.com/a/11841648/9787887</a> and run the example below to see the difference between being a block container vs merely an inline-box.</p>
<p><div class="snippet" data-lang="js" data-hide="false" data-console="true" data-babel="false" data-babel-preset-react="false" data-babel-preset-ts="false">
<div class="snippet-code">
<pre class="snippet-code-css lang-css prettyprint-override"><code>div {
  margin: 5px;
  padding: 5px;
  border: 2px solid black;
  line-height: 100px;
}
.flex-container {
  border-color: blue;
  display: flex;
}
span {
  line-height: 15px;
}</code></pre>
<pre class="snippet-code-html lang-html prettyprint-override"><code>&lt;div&gt;
    &lt;span&gt;
      inline level box (doesn't have own linebox), instead &lt;br/&gt;
      participates in the div's linebox, hence 100px "perceived line-height"
    &lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;div class="flex-container"&gt;
    &lt;span&gt;
      blockified block container, establishes its own block formatting context &lt;br/&gt;
      has own lineboxes, hence "perceived line-height" is 15px
    &lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;</code></pre>
</div>
</div>
</p>
<p>Also, you can also see more here: <a href="https://www.w3.org/TR/css-display-3/#the-display-properties" rel="nofollow noreferrer">https://www.w3.org/TR/css-display-3/#the-display-properties</a></p>
<p><code>display: flex</code> <code>display: block</code> etc are actually shorthand syntaxes for more comprehensive <code>&lt;display-outside&gt; &lt;display-inside&gt;</code> under the hood.</p>

Q: CMake ARCHIVE_CREATE with relative paths A: <p>For anyone that might come across this: version 3.31 added the <code>WORKING_DIRECTORY</code> option. According to <a href="https://cmake.org/cmake/help/latest/command/file.html#archive-create" rel="nofollow noreferrer">the docs</a>:<br />
<em>&quot;Specify the directory in which the archive creation operation will be executed. Paths in the <code>&lt;paths&gt;</code> argument can be relative to this directory. If this option is not provided, the current working directory will be used by default.&quot;</em></p>
<p>So now you could write:</p>
<pre><code>file(ARCHIVE_CREATE
  OUTPUT &quot;${CMAKE_CURRENT_BINARY_DIR}/bundle.zip&quot;
  PATHS &quot;${CMAKE_CURRENT_SOURCE_DIR}/assets/demo&quot;
  WORKING_DIRECTORY &quot;${CMAKE_CURRENT_SOURCE_DIR}/assets/demo&quot;
  FORMAT &quot;zip&quot;
  VERBOSE
)
</code></pre>
<p>PS: the PR that added this option was mine 😬</p>
